## Architecture - How the single RMAP.lib is assembled (current design)

Each module has its own `CMakeLists.txt` (pulled in by `add_subdirectory` from the
top-level file), so each becomes **its own Visual Studio project** (and its own target in
every other generator).

- **Core** is `add_library(RMAP STATIC ...)`. This target **is** the final `RMAP.lib`.
- **Each service** is `add_library(<name> OBJECT ...)`. The top-level file folds each
  enabled service's compiled objects into the core archive with
  `target_sources(RMAP PRIVATE $<TARGET_OBJECTS:<name>>)`. Because `$<TARGET_OBJECTS>`
  adds objects as *sources* (not a link dependency), the deliverable stays one archive,
  each object appears exactly once, and the install/export set is cleanly just `RMAP`.
- Turning a service option OFF skips its `add_subdirectory` entirely, so its project
  disappears from the generated solution and its objects never enter `RMAP.lib`. This is
  the CMake-driven include/exclude DavidSr asked for.

### KEY DECISION - target shapes (core STATIC + services OBJECT), 4 projects

This session's restructure chose **core = one STATIC target named `RMAP`** (which is the
archive) plus **services = OBJECT libraries folded via `$<TARGET_OBJECTS>`**. Rationale:

- This yields **exactly four module projects** (`RMAP`, `RMAP_Svc_SB`, `RMAP_Svc_Rest`,
  `RMAP_Svc_SocketIO`), matching DavidSr's "separate project files for each project".
- An earlier documented design (see History) made **core** an OBJECT library too
  (`RMAP_Objects`) and added a *separate* final `RMAP` STATIC target that folded ALL
  four object libraries. That works but produces **five** projects and makes core appear
  twice (as `RMAP_Objects` and `RMAP`). The current shape avoids that duplication.
- Do NOT switch services to `target_link_libraries(RMAP PUBLIC <objlib>)`: that records
  the object library in `RMAP`'s `INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES`, forcing every object lib
  into the `install(EXPORT)` set (CMake errors) and installing stray artifacts.

### KEY DECISION - services see core headers without a link dependency

Each service reads core's include dirs and compile definitions via
`$<TARGET_PROPERTY:RMAP,INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES>` and
`$<TARGET_PROPERTY:RMAP,INTERFACE_COMPILE_DEFINITIONS>` (added PRIVATE), **not** via
`target_link_libraries(<service> PRIVATE RMAP)`. Reason: `$<TARGET_OBJECTS:<service>>`
already makes `RMAP` depend on the service (build order); linking the service to `RMAP`
would create a dependency **cycle**. Reading target properties pulls the usage
requirements at generate time without any dependency edge. (Generator expressions in the
property, e.g. `$<INSTALL_INTERFACE:...>`, evaluate empty during build - fine.)
This also gives services core's public include dir and, transitively, the nlohmann_json
include (json include is added PUBLIC to `RMAP` at the top level).

**A service with real source in subfolders must also add its OWN dirs.** While a service
was a flat placeholder (one `.cpp` directly in `src/`), quoted includes like
`#include "pch.h"` resolved relative to the file's own folder, so no self-include-dir was
needed. Once `RMAP_Svc_SB` gained files in `src/base/`, `src/client/`, etc., those files'
`#include "pch.h"` (and `"Library.h"`) could no longer find headers sitting in `src/`.
Fix (applied to `RMAP_Svc_SB`, mirroring core): add
`${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/include/RMAP_Svc_SB` and `${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/src` as
PRIVATE include dirs (before the core `$<TARGET_PROPERTY>` entry). The same self include
dirs were added to `RMAP_Svc_Rest` this session when it got real source + PCH (even though
its `src/` is currently flat, the PCH force-include and `#include "pch.h"` still need `src`
on the include path). When `SocketIO` gets real source, add the equivalent self include
dirs to it too.

### KEY DECISION - filters via source_group(TREE), disk-mirrored

Each module's `CMakeLists.txt` calls `source_group(TREE ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR} FILES
${<Module>_FILES})`, producing Solution Explorer filters that mirror the on-disk folders:
`src`, `src\base`, `src\core`, `src\mem`, `src\model`, `include\RMAP`. **Headers are
listed as target sources** (in the `*_FILES` list) so they show in the project and get
filtered too. CMake also auto-adds `Source Files`, `Object Libraries` (the folded service
`.obj`s under `RMAP`), and `Precompile Header File` filters.

DavidSr accepted disk-mirrored filter names (`src\base`) over the old hand-authored
prefixed style (`Source Files\Base` / `Header Files\...`). If he later wants the prefixed
style, switch to two `source_group(TREE ... PREFIX "Source Files"/"Header Files" ...)`
calls split by extension.

### Third-party dependencies - the shared fetch/system pattern

All third-party libs follow one pattern in the **top-level** `CMakeLists.txt`: an
`option(RMAP_USE_SYSTEM_<NAME> ... OFF)`; when OFF, `ExternalProject_Add(<name>_fetch ...)`
git-clones the (header-only) lib at **build** time into `RMAP/third_party/<name>/` (each
gitignored, not committed), `add_dependencies(RMAP <name>_fetch)`, and adds the lib's
include dir to `RMAP` as `$<BUILD_INTERFACE:...>` PUBLIC; when ON, `find_package(<name>
<ver> CONFIG REQUIRED)` + `target_link_libraries(RMAP PUBLIC <name>::<name>)`. The
`if(EXISTS "${dir}/.git")` guard in each block passes empty git args when the source is
already present (skip re-download).

**All five ExternalProjects set `UPDATE_DISCONNECTED ON`** (added 2026-07-24). Without it, a
git-based ExternalProject re-runs its **update step on every build** ("Performing update
step... Already at requested tag"); that update regenerates its stamp, and since the
configure step depends on the update stamp, it **cascades** into re-configure -> re-build ->
re-install every single build (all ending "Up-to-date", but noisy and slow, especially for
the compiled curl/BoringSSL). `UPDATE_DISCONNECTED ON` skips the automatic update after the
initial clone, breaking the cascade - verified: after one regenerate, a second
`cmake --build build --target curl_build` is a pure no-op (just the MSBuild banner). Note
the guard above does NOT fix this on its own: the buildsystem bakes in the git update step
at the generate where the source was first cloned (`.git` didn't exist yet), so it keeps
running until reconfigured; `UPDATE_DISCONNECTED` is the actual fix. To intentionally pull a
newer upstream (bump a tag, refresh BoringSSL's moving `main`), run the generated
`<name>-update` target (e.g. `cmake --build build --target curl_build-update`) or delete the
dep's stamp dir and rebuild. TRADE-OFF for BoringSSL specifically: it tracks the moving
`main` branch, so disconnecting freezes it at the currently-cloned commit until manually
updated - accepted (better reproducibility); the four tag-pinned deps never needed updates
anyway. Three **header-only** libs are wired this way:
nlohmann_json, asio, websocketpp. All three include dirs are PUBLIC on `RMAP`, so the
service OBJECT libraries see them transitively via
`$<TARGET_PROPERTY:RMAP,INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES>`. Two further libs, **BoringSSL
and libcurl, are COMPILED** (not header-only) and therefore use a different, heavier
block (real configure+build+install via ExternalProject, PRIVATE link) - see their
subsections below.

