  build (`C:\Dev\OMB\Sneeze\deps\builds\windows-x64\{release,debug}\libs\curl\install\lib`),
  NOT guessed: Windows `libcurl.lib` (Release) / `libcurl-d.lib` (Debug, curl's `-d` debug
  postfix); Unix `libcurl.a`. Encoded as `BUILD_BYPRODUCTS`/link path with
  `libcurl$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-d>${CMAKE_STATIC_LIBRARY_SUFFIX}` on Windows and
  `${CMAKE_STATIC_LIBRARY_PREFIX}curl${CMAKE_STATIC_LIBRARY_SUFFIX}` elsewhere. (Sneeze's
  `FindSneezeCurl.cmake` also lists older `libcurl_a`/`libcurl_a-d` names, but 8.9.1's
  static MSVC build produces plain `libcurl`/`libcurl-d` - confirmed on disk.)
- **Linkage = PRIVATE** (internal, same rationale as BoringSSL): `target_link_libraries(
  RMAP PRIVATE <curl lib>)`; on Windows also `ws2_32 wldap32 crypt32 normaliz` (curl's
  Windows/Schannel system deps, from Sneeze's "curl Windows deps" list). Kept out of the
  export set; consequence identical to BoringSSL - a final exe linking `RMAP::RMAP` must
  also link libcurl + those system libs.
- **Header visibility + CURL_STATICLIB:** curl include dir added build-only PUBLIC
  (`$<BUILD_INTERFACE:${RMAP_CURL_PREFIX}/include>`) so services can `#include <curl/curl.h>`
  via RMAP's INTERFACE include dirs. `target_compile_definitions(RMAP PUBLIC
  $<BUILD_INTERFACE:CURL_STATICLIB>)` - REQUIRED by every TU including `<curl/curl.h>` when
  linking a static libcurl; wrapped in `$<BUILD_INTERFACE:>` so it does not leak into the
  exported interface (build-only, like the include dir).
- **Build prereqs:** just the C/C++ compiler - libcurl adds NO new toolchain requirement
  beyond BoringSSL's (and on Windows it uses Schannel, so no BoringSSL either). On a clean
  build curl fetches + compiles from source (a few minutes, like BoringSSL).
- `curl_build` was added to the service dependency loop (see next section).

### KEY DECISION - services must depend on the fetch/build targets (clean-build ordering)

Only `RMAP` depends on the `*_fetch` targets, but the service OBJECT libraries compile
**before** `RMAP` (their objects fold into it via `$<TARGET_OBJECTS>`) and are the code
that actually `#include`s the fetched headers. On a **clean** build a service could compile
before its headers finished cloning -> `C1083`. Fix (top-level, after the fetch/build
blocks): loop over `RMAP_Svc_SB/Rest/SocketIO` and, for each that exists, `add_dependencies`
on each existing dep target (`nlohmann_json_fetch`, `asio_fetch`, `websocketpp_fetch`,
`boringssl_build`, and `curl_build` - the last two so services wait for the compiled libs'
installed headers too).
Verified: after deleting both `build/` and `RMAP/third_party/`, a cold configure+build
fetches all three and compiles services with no header/`not a git repository`/`MSB8066`
errors. (The `not a git repository` error seen during testing was a false alarm from
deleting only the source dirs while leaving ExternalProject's download stamps in `build/`.)

### Install / find_package (top-level, unchanged by the restructure)

`install(TARGETS RMAP EXPORT RMAPTargets ARCHIVE DESTINATION lib)`, per-module
`install(DIRECTORY <Module>/include/ DESTINATION include)` (guarded by each option),
`install(EXPORT RMAPTargets NAMESPACE RMAP:: ...)`, and
`configure_package_config_file` + `write_basic_package_version_file` using
`cmake/RMAPConfig.cmake.in`. Downstream: `find_package(RMAP CONFIG REQUIRED)` +
`target_link_libraries(... RMAP::RMAP)`. Package config installs to `lib/cmake/RMAP`.

### MSVC settings

Core (and each service, for symmetry/future real code) applies under `if(MSVC)`:
`UNICODE _UNICODE _LIB` (PRIVATE), `/permissive- /sdl /W3`. Core additionally sets
`INTERPROCEDURAL_OPTIMIZATION_RELEASE ON`. Core uses PCH
(`target_precompile_headers(RMAP PRIVATE src/pch.h)`). **`RMAP_Svc_SB` now also uses PCH**
(`target_precompile_headers(RMAP_Svc_SB PRIVATE src/pch.h)`, placed after the `if(MSVC)`
block, before `source_group` - matching core) since real SB code with a `pch` pair
landed. Note: CMake force-includes the PCH header into every TU on MSVC (`/FI` via the
generated `cmake_pch`), so sources need not manually `#include "pch.h"` (16 of the SB
`.cpp`s do anyway; the include guard makes that harmless, and `RMAP_Svc_SB.cpp` which does
not include it still compiles fine). **`RMAP_Svc_Rest` now also uses PCH**
(`target_precompile_headers(RMAP_Svc_Rest PRIVATE src/pch.h)`) since real REST source with
a `pch` pair landed this session; its `CMakeLists.txt` also gained the self include dirs
(`include/RMAP_Svc_Rest` + `src`) so `pch.h` resolves - same fix pattern as SB. REST did
NOT get SB's `WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN`/`NOCRYPT` defines: those exist solely for the BoringSSL
x `<wincrypt.h>` collision, and a grep confirmed the REST sources use no asio/websocketpp/
BoringSSL (so no `<windows.h>`-via-asio pull, no collision). `RMAP_Svc_SocketIO` still has
**no PCH** (placeholder has no `pch.h`) - add per-service PCH matching core when real code
lands there too.

